{"person_id":"candidate-1854-inbee-park","name":"Inbee Park","cohort_group":"Athletes","category":"Golfer","birth_year":1988,"age_at_milestone":19,"milestone_by_age_26":"Won the 2008 U.S. Women's Open at age 19, becoming the youngest winner in the tournament's history and claiming her first LPGA victory and first major championship.","early_history_summary":"Park began playing golf at age 10 in Seoul, inspired by watching Se Ri Pak win the 1998 U.S. Women's Open on television. At 12, she moved to the United States with her mother and sister to pursue golf, training at a golf academy in Florida. She won the 2002 U.S. Girls' Junior at 14, was a five-time AJGA All-American with nine AJGA wins, and turned professional at 17 in 2006, earning LPGA Tour status for 2007.","primary_leverage_engine":"Early specialization","normalized_primary_engine":"Early specialization / prior reps","secondary_engine":"Technical depth","started_serious_reps_before_20_score":1,"prior_reps_score":3,"scarce_skill_depth_score":2,"native_distribution_score":0,"elite_ecosystem_network_score":1,"complementary_team_score":0,"structural_wave_score":2,"concentration_intensity_score":2,"capital_safety_score":1,"domain_proximity_score":2,"total_leverage_score":14,"success_tier":2,"leverage_evidence_confidence":"High","family_context_summary":"Born in Seoul, South Korea; father ran a business in Seoul while mother moved with Inbee and her sister to the US to support her golf career. Family had sufficient means to relocate internationally.","parent_family_domain_summary":"Not documented in reviewed sources; no family golf background noted.","early_family_financial_platform_support_score":1,"parent_family_domain_advantage_score":0,"inherited_audience_business_network_score":0,"elite_institution_performance_pipeline_score":1,"frontier_geography_ecosystem_score":1,"rare_early_tools_facilities_score":1,"dedicated_mentor_coach_tutor_score":1,"exceptional_peer_cofounder_sibling_score":0,"early_online_platform_community_score":0,"direct_customer_domain_exposure_score":2,"prodigy_physical_edge_score":1,"adversity_constraint_catalyst_score":1,"inherited_access_stack_count":7,"exceptional_inherited_access_count":0,"all_documented_early_condition_count":7,"primary_early_advantage_archetype":"Self-created domain repetition","primary_early_advantage_tags":"early specialization, family relocation, golf academy, Korean golf wave, AJGA circuit","evidence_summary":"Park was inspired to play golf at 10 after watching Se Ri Pak win the 1998 U.S. Women's Open. Her family made significant sacrifices to support her career, with her mother and sister relocating from Seoul to Florida while her father remained in Korea to run his business. She trained at a golf academy and won the U.S. Girls' Junior at 14. After turning professional at 17, she won the 2008 U.S. Women's Open at 19, the youngest champion in tournament history. Her early advantages included family support for international relocation, immersion in Florida's golf ecosystem, and the broader Korean golf wave.","early_advantage_evidence_confidence":"High","source_count":4,"primary_source_url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inbee_Park","source_urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inbee_Park","https://www.nytimes.com/2013/09/11/sports/golf/with-hours-of-practice-and-a-little-flour-on-her-face-park-grew-into-a-star.html","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Park-In-Bee","https://www.usga.org/articles/2015/12/usga-success-big-part-of-park-s-hall-of-fame-resume.html"],"annotation_status":"subagent_researched_beta","source_audit_status":"not_independently_audited","data_version":"0.3.0-trajectory-beta","eligibility_status":"age_26_eligible","starting_point":"Born in Seoul, South Korea in 1988; father ran a business in Seoul, mother relocated with Inbee and her sister to Florida to support her golf career.","current_position":"Retired from professional LPGA Tour (last competed August 2022); LPGA Hall of Famer with 21 career titles and 7 major championships; mother of two; involved in IOC Athletes' Commission and IGF board.","current_position_year":2025,"is_living":true,"trajectory":[{"year":2002,"age":14,"event":"Won the U.S. Girls' Junior Championship, establishing herself as a junior golf phenom"},{"year":2006,"age":17,"event":"Turned professional, playing on the Duramed Futures Tour after LPGA denied age waiver request"},{"year":2008,"age":19,"event":"Won the U.S. Women's Open at Interlachen, becoming the youngest champion in tournament history"},{"year":2013,"age":24,"event":"Won first three LPGA majors of the season, becoming only the second player to achieve the feat"},{"year":2016,"age":27,"event":"Won Olympic gold medal at Rio Olympics and qualified for LPGA Hall of Fame"},{"year":2022,"age":34,"event":"Made final LPGA Tour appearance at the AIG Women's British Open before stepping away from competitive golf"}]}
